"""p2_asdu.py -- generated by gen_asdu.py; do not hand-edit.

The ASDU structure catalog, embedded so a reader needs nothing but this package
to turn a P2 body into named fields. `p2_data.py` names operations and decodes
enums; this says what the bytes of a body are.

Everything here is field order, field type and field width -- the same content
the reference tables in PROTOCOL.md carry, in machine-readable form. The walker
that consumes it is `p2_body.py`, which is hand-written and not generated.

  STRUCTS      {structure: [[field, type], ...]}
  NESTED       {"Parent::type": [[field, type], ...]} -- one name can mean
               different things under different parents, so these are kept
               parent-qualified rather than merged
  WIDTHS       {type: bytes}
  TEXTLIKE     types carried as a string TLV (tag, u16 BE length, bytes)
  SIGNED       types whose fixed-width value is two's-complement
  ALIAS        arm-name spellings that differ from their structure's name
  REALW        real_addr_ width, by the Physical_address_* that encloses it
  ALARM        Alarm_object arm width, by tag
  POINT_TAGS   All_points tag -> arm
  CHOICE_TAGS  {choice: {"complete": bool, "map": {tag: arm}}}
  OPS          {opcode: (request structure or None, response structure or None)}
  OP_TAILS     {("req"|"rsp", opcode): [[field, type], ...]} -- fields the
               structure library does not declare that the wire nevertheless
               carries, consistently, for that one operation. Kept separate from
               STRUCTS so that what is DECLARED stays distinguishable from what
               is OBSERVED. Each was identified by content, not fitted to a
               length, and each rescues every body of its opcode: without them
               342 bodies stop one or two bytes short. See PROTOCOL.md 10.1.
  STRUCT_SKIPS {"Struct.field": n} -- undeclared bytes that sit BETWEEN two
               declared fields rather than after the last. One entry,
               `AP2_Cabinet_Display_Response`, whose constant `0x21` is the only
               thing between its declared tail and a clean walk of all 60.
"""

